- How would you handle allowing at most k blocks and a penalty for unused blocks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a two-dimensional DP with an inner precomputation and path reconstruction, a common interview pattern for text/partition problems.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic edit-cost precomputation (costPal).
- Partition DP with exactly k segments.
- Reconstruction of cut points and time/space trade-offs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPalPartitionCost(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const costPal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      if (i >= j) costPal[i][j] = 0;
      else costPal[i][j] = costPal[i + 1][j - 1] + (s[i] !== s[j] ? 1 : 0);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let x = 1; x <= n; x++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let i = t - 1; i < x; i++) {
        const val = dp[i][t - 1] + costPal[i][x - 1];
        if (val < dp[x][t]) {
          dp[x][t] = val;
          par[x][t] = i;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction can follow from par
  return { cost: dp[n][k], cuts: par };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt for at most k substrings instead of exactly k?
- What optimizations reduce time to O(n^2) or enable pruning of the i-loop?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem assesses your ability to handle non-trivial path constraints in dynamic programming, specifically exact turn counting. It also requires path reconstruction, reflecting real-world interview complexity where identifying a solution isn't sufficient—you must also provide the actual path.

## Key Concepts
- **4D DP state** capturing both position and path history: `(i, j, t, dir)` tracks where you are, how many turns you've made, and your incoming direction
- **Turn counting** via conditional transitions: staying in the same direction doesn't increment the turn count, while switching does
- **Backpointer-based reconstruction** stores the predecessor for each state, enabling full path recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [-Infinity, -Infinity])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [null, null])
    )
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromRight = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromDown = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : -Infinity;
          if (fromRight >= fromDown) {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t, 0];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t-1, 1];
          }
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromDown = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromRight = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : -Infinity;
          if (fromDown >= fromRight) {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t, 1];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t-1, 0];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const endDir = dp[n-1][m-1][k][0] >= dp[n-1][m-1][k][1] ? 0 : 1;
  const maxSum = dp[n-1][m-1][k][endDir];
  const path = [];
  let cur = [n-1, m-1, k, endDir];

  while (cur) {
    path.unshift([cur[0], cur[1]]);
    cur = parent[cur[0]][cur[1]][cur[2]][cur[3]];
  }

  return { sum: maxSum, path };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real DP design with an extra state (discount used) and careful recurrence.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP with a binary state (discount used or not)
- Transitions combine taking cost with/without discount
- Space optimization using rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostWithDiscount(grid){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  let dp0 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  let dp1 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  dp0[0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0] = Math.floor(grid[0][0] / 2);
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
    dp0[j] = grid[0][j] + dp0[j-1];
    dp1[j] = Math.min(grid[0][j] + dp1[j-1], Math.floor(grid[0][j]/2) + dp0[j-1]);
  }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    dp0[0] += grid[i][0];
    dp1[0] = Math.min(grid[i][0] + dp1[0], Math.floor(grid[i][0]/2) + dp0[0-1]);
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const v = grid[i][j];
      dp1[j] = Math.min(v + Math.min(dp1[j], dp1[j-1]), Math.floor(v/2) + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]));
      dp0[j] = v + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]);
    }
  }
  return dp1[m-1];
}
```

